# Keycloak — Application Auth (cloudless.gr)

How the **app** authenticates and authorizes against Keycloak. For admin-console / REST
operations (creating users, editing clients) see the **keycloak-ops** skill instead.

## The model in one paragraph

OIDC **Authorization Code + PKCE**, brokered by **next-auth v5** against Keycloak
(`auth.cloudless.gr`, realm `master`, client `cloudless-app`). The browser never sees a
password — it authenticates on Keycloak's hosted page. next-auth stores an **encrypted JWT
cookie** (`authjs.session-token`) holding the Keycloak `access_token` / `id_token` /
`refresh_token`. Page routes are gated in `src/proxy.ts`; API routes are gated in
`src/lib/api-auth.ts`. **Admin = membership in the Keycloak `admin` group** (`groups`
claim) or an `admin` realm role (`realm_access.roles`).

## The files and their contract

| File | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `src/lib/auth.ts` | next-auth v5 config: Keycloak provider, `jwt()` callback (decode `groups` + `realm_access.roles`, refresh rotation), `session()` callback, RP-initiated logout in `events.signOut`. |
| `src/app/api/auth/[...nextauth]/route.ts` | Exposes next-auth `GET`/`POST` handlers. |
| `src/lib/keycloak-auth.ts` | Client-side shim (amplify-compatible surface) — `signIn`/`signOut`/`signUp` delegate to next-auth / Keycloak hosted pages. |
| `src/proxy.ts` | **Page** gate: `readAuthToken()` via `getToken()` on the session cookie; `/admin/**` needs the `admin` group, `/dashboard/**` needs any session. |
| `src/lib/api-auth.ts` | **API** gate: `requireAuth` / `requireAdmin`. Verifies the Keycloak access token's RS256 signature against the realm JWKS; `isAdmin()` reads `groups` / `realm_access.roles`. |
| `src/context/AuthContext.tsx` | Reads `/api/auth/session`; calls admin APIs with `Authorization: Bearer <session.accessToken>`. |

## api-auth.ts — the canonical contract (DO copy this shape)

```ts
// Verify Keycloak access tokens against the realm JWKS; authorize by group/role.
const KC_ISSUER = (process.env.KEYCLOAK_ISSUER ?? process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_KEYCLOAK_ISSUER ?? "")
  .replace(/\/+$/, "");

// JWKS: local set from KEYCLOAK_JWKS_JSON (offline/dev) else remote realm certs.
const getJWKS = KC_ISSUER
  ? createRemoteJWKSet(new URL(`${KC_ISSUER}/protocol/openid-connect/certs`))
  : null;

export async function verifyToken(token: string) {
  if (getJWKS) {
    const { payload } = await jwtVerify(token, getJWKS, { issuer: KC_ISSUER });
    return payload;           // NOTE: do NOT enforce audience — KC aud is often "account"
  }
  // dev/test fallback: decode + expiry only (no signature) when no issuer configured
}

export function isAdmin(d) {
  const groups = d?.groups ?? d?.["cognito:groups"] ?? [];   // cognito:* kept for back-compat
  if (groups.includes("admin")) return true;
  const roles = d?.realm_access?.roles ?? [];
  return roles.includes("admin") || roles.includes("realm:admin");
}
```

### Rules / gotchas (these are the mistakes to avoid)

1. **Verify the signature.** Always `jwtVerify` against the JWKS. The only no-signature
   path is the explicit dev/test fallback that triggers **only** when `KEYCLOAK_ISSUER` is
   unset. Never ship a guard that decodes without verifying when an issuer is present.
2. **Read the right claim.** Admin is `groups` (Keycloak group-membership mapper) or
   `realm_access.roles`. It is **not** `cognito:groups` (legacy; honored only for
   back-compat during transition).
3. **Do not enforce `audience`.** Keycloak access tokens commonly carry `aud: "account"`;
   enforcing audience rejects valid tokens. Enforce `issuer` only.
4. **`groups` requires a protocol mapper.** The `cloudless-app` client must have a
   **Group Membership** mapper (claim `groups`, added to ID + access token, `full.path`
   false). Without it the claim is absent and every admin check fails. Run
   `pnpm keycloak:apply` (or the doctor below) to assert it.
5. **Client sends the access token.** `AuthContext` calls admin APIs with
   `Authorization: Bearer ${session.accessToken}`. The session exposes `accessToken` via
   the next-auth `session()` callback — keep that wired.
6. **Adding a protected API route:** `const auth = await requireAdmin(request); if (!auth.ok) return auth.response;`
   (or `requireAuth` for non-admin). 401 = no/invalid token, 403 = valid token without admin.

## Verify the implementation is correctly applied

```bash
# Static config (realm/client/group/mapper/redirect URIs):
pnpm keycloak:doctor          # tools/keycloak-cli/keycloak.sh doctor — read-only checks
pnpm keycloak:apply --dry-run # scripts/keycloak-apply.ts — shows drift
pnpm keycloak:apply           # idempotently fix client/group/mapper config

# Unit contract:
pnpm exec vitest run __tests__/api-auth.test.ts

# End-to-end login (requires e2e users — see test-accounts doc):
E2E_BASE_URL=https://cloudless.gr pnpm e2e:prod
```

## Common failures → cause

| Symptom | Likely cause |
| --- | --- |
| All admin APIs 403 for a real admin | `groups` mapper missing on `cloudless-app`, or user not in `admin` group |
| Admin APIs 401 with a valid login | issuer mismatch (`KEYCLOAK_ISSUER` drift) or JWKS unreachable from the pod/Lambda |
| Works locally, 401 in prod | `KEYCLOAK_ISSUER` not set in that runtime; verifyToken silently took the dev fallback and now a real JWKS rejects |
| `redirect_uri` invalid at login | `cloudless-app` redirectUris missing `<origin>/api/auth/callback/keycloak` — see keycloak-ops |

